Respect setgid parent directories in secure_parent_dir

A setgid bit on a directory never appears by accident -- it is an
explicit admin declaration of intentional group sharing (e.g. a
multi-agent setup where a second assistant reads the same memories).
Forcing such a directory to 0700 on every auth token refresh breaks
that contract. The secret files inside keep their own 0600 modes.

--- a/hermes_constants.py
+++ b/hermes_constants.py
@@ -1024,6 +1024,11 @@
     if parent == Path("/") or len(parent.parts) < 3:
         return
     try:
+        # A setgid parent is an explicit admin declaration of intentional
+        # group sharing; tightening it to 0700 would break that contract.
+        # The secret files inside keep their own restrictive modes.
+        if parent.stat().st_mode & 0o2000:
+            return
         os.chmod(parent, 0o700)
     except OSError:
         pass
